Z||000000||mrgone||04-27-2001||09:38 PM||||called the insurance company and they said id be about 350 a month or 4200 a year....<BR>then after 3 months when im passed my 19th birthday it will go down 75$ to 275$ per month or 3300$ year<P>whats with that?<BR>i havent had any claims yet<BR>and my driving record is semi-clean (1 ticket)||||reg||8
Z||000001||Spektyr||04-27-2001||09:42 PM||||Well, I'm 26 so I get a pretty good break on the insurance.  With the same driving record (1 ticket) my insurance is pretty cheap.<P>Liability only the Lincoln runs 60 a month, add the Fiero for a total of 92 a month.  Granted, I'm using a borderline fly-by-night company and insuring more than one car.  Once my Fiero starts getting close to the way Pontiac should have made them, I'll probably switch companies and get full-coverage on it, which will undoubtedly cost more.<P>When I had full-coverage on my 1990 Nissan PU in '95 and a considerably worse driving record it was running me 300 a month and I was 19-20.  I had half a dozen speeding tickets and 3 accidents on my record at the time.  The only thing that saved me was I was in the military and didn't drive the truck to work.  I lived on base and just walked.<P>My advice with insurance companies... well it's not entirely 'legal'.  Suffer from acute amnesia and poor depth perception.  You don't recall any tickets or accidents and are sure you drive less than 5 miles to work.  It's their job to assess the correct amount of money to screw you out of, let them do the police record search.  "Oh yeah, I forgot about that ticket..."<P>Everything else is based on car make, year, gender, age, and zip code.  Yup, WHERE you live makes a difference on how much it costs.  I've actually selected an apartment simply because insurance was cheaper in that part of town.  I had to drive halfway across town to go to work, but I still told them it was only 5 miles.  If they ever checked up on it (which they don't normally) I'd just tell them it felt like 5 miles to me, my mistake.<P>I think the concept of making me pay money "Just in case" I wreck my car is a little silly.  Not refunding my money if I don't wreck my car ticks me off.  So basically, if you're a good driver they get paid to do nothing.  IMHO I should get my money back if I switch companies and didn't use their service (ie file a claim).  So I don't feel bad if I bend the rules a bit.  Or a lot.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||04-27-2001||09:50 PM||||wow !!!!!! im glad now im not a teen anymore. I dont even pay $400 a year and mine is insured for $20K with $250 deduct, and I mark more than 15,000 miles per year on ap.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||04-27-2001||10:01 PM||||I'm 20 with perfect driving record so far. For the most basic barely legal insurance for my Fieros, I pay $98/car, which equals 196/month. I'm with StateFarm, and have multiple dicsounts. Without the discounts, I would be near $110-$115/month/car.||||reg||1
