Z||000014||fyerstarter||04-28-2001||04:39 AM||||Insurance is a scam. For the average driver under 21 (with an average driving record and a sports car) insurance will prob be around $2,000 a year for liability. Most of these policies will only cover damages up to around $10,000 - $15,000<P>Now lets say you drive from the time your are 16-21 and you get in one major accident during that time. Statistics will show this is more accidents than the average person will have during time frame.<P>Now, no matter how bad the accident is your liability insurance will only cover $10,00 - $15,000<P>Now let's do a little math here. <P>you pay $2,000 a year<BR>for 6 years<P>which is a total of $12,000<P>So <B>IF</B> in fact you did get in an accident during that time then you have done nothing more than pay the damages in advance.<P>If do not get in an accident then you have simply donated 12 grand to the insurance companies.<P>Insurance companies are in the business of making money. They do not provide a service in any way shape or form. <P>If you had no insurance and got in an accident here is what would happen (least here in California where laws are pretty strict)<P>-You will be fined a $1000 fine<BR>-You will need to get insurance<BR>-You will be responsible for the damages of the accident (of course this will take a long time for the other party to collect from you and when they do it will be done in payments, hmmmm ... sounds almost like insurance payments to me. Except, you only have to pay them if you actually <B>do</B> get in an accident!<P>One of these days when we can get all of the politicians out of the pockets of the special interest groups then insurance will hopefully become a federally mandated, regulated, and taxed function, Just like licensing and registration. <P>You pay a small amount out of your taxes every year, you pay a reasonable yearly renewal fee, you get your insurance like everything else. But in this case you will only need to pay enough to keep the system alive and not a fill the pockets of greedy insurance companies. <P>I don't see how something can be made a law (like insurance) and then not have any restrictions placed on price. In a free market economy there is competition to keep the prices down, but the minute the product becomes required by law then the demand for the product is infinite and they can charge whatever they want.<P>Just look how much insurance rates have risin in the last 5 years. The only reason that they can get away with this is because we let them. Do you want to be paying $500 a year in another 5 years for insurance?<P>What can we do about this?<P>Sorry for ranting but insurance is just one of the many things that pisses me off  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||fyerstarter||04-28-2001||05:51 AM||||How does this sound as a viable alternative to insurance?<P>A Federally Mandated Insurance Savings Account<P>-When you first recieve your license you begin paying into a savings account at a rate of $150 a month<P>-You pay into this account until it accumulates $15,000 (an acceptable amount of liability coverage in most states)<P>-During this time the money you put into this account accumulates interest and the government or the bank (whoever is holding this money) can use the money to invest and other such activities so that they may get a return on your money as well<P>-Once your savings account reaches the $15,000 mark you will begin to be payed back the interest once a year (like a tax return) keeping the balance at $15,000. Or, you can choose to leave the money in the account in order to accumulate more interest per year on the larger sum.<P>-If you get in an accident and it is legally proven to be your fault the damages will automatically be deducted from your account. Any damages above $15k will be taken up between you and the other party in private (and most likely in court) , just as it would with the liability coverage you have now.<P>-If your account balance drops below the 15k limit because of an accident then you must start paying your monthly payments again until it is back up. You will continue to get interest on your balance but will not recieve payments on it till it's back up to 15 grand.<P>-When you get too old or dont want to drive anymore you just turn in your license and get all your money back<P><BR>Now lets take this scenario:<P>-At 16 years old you start paying into your account at a rate of $150 a month<P>-By the time you reach age 24 (considered a much safer driver anyway) you will have satisfied your $1500 requirement and payments will stop<P>-For the 8 years you have been paying into the account you will have been compounding interest at a rate of say 3%<P>Year 1  $54<BR>Year 2  $109.62<BR>Year 3  $166.90<BR>Year 4  $225.91<BR>Year 5  $286.69<BR>Year 6  $349.29<BR>Year 7  $413.77<BR>Year 8  $480.18<P>Total Balance:  $16,486.36<P>So on your 24th birthday your insurance payments stop and you recieve a check for $1,486.36 for the interest on your money for the past 8 years.<P>You now have $15,000 in an account for liability reasons which you cannot touch and which will be automatically deducted from in case of an at-fault accident<P>You will recieve a yearly check on the interest of your money in the amount of $450 or you can choose to leave it in there and make even more interest<P>As long as you do not get in an accident then you never have to pay another insurance bill in your life.<P>In the case that you do get in a wreck the damages are automatically deducted and you will need to resume payments. <P>Your financial istitution will make money of of your money while it is in their posession, you will make money off of interest, you will have sufficient funds to cover an accident should it happen, and after the initial amount is built up you will no longer have any monthly payments. Plus you get a nice fat bonus when you get old and stop driving. <P>NOW! I know your asking "Well you're just assuming that this kid isn't some wreckless maniac getting in an accident every few months!!"<P>Well here is the thing. Every year the insurance companies make hundreds of millions of dollars in profit. There is a point at which they could just break even and have enough $$ to cover all liabilities and still not go in debt.<P>This is the amount we should be paying. <P>For every reckless driver there are 5 more safe drivers paying the same amount, this is what the insurance companies bank on to make a profit.<P>So I say we reduce the amount those 5 people are paying to just the amount needed to support the sytem and send these greedy bastards to the unemployment line!<P>I'm sure there may be flaws in my math and my interest rates may not be accurate or feasable but it's 2:30am and I think it's a pretty damn good plan. <P>Now elect me president and I'll put it in motion as my first order of business!!!<P>....after I find myself a nice intern   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Good night all<P><p>[This message has been edited by fyerstarter (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||LivoniaFieroGT||04-28-2001||10:01 PM||||After checking out several national insurance companies....  AAA was the only one that didn't list the Fiero as a high performance car.  Most others even classified the 4 cylinder as such. They had the best rates for me.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Tryxalon||04-28-2001||10:21 PM||||As bad as it is, I would prefer and trust the tender mercies of a crass comercialistic capitalist Insurance company over a Government program. <P>Remember: Income tax was SUPPOSED to be 3% on the richest 5% of the population!! (The Tax Code being a perfect example of <BR>a "Government Program"). <P>Plus the fact that my current coverage covers me for over $2,000,000 responsibility (and that is in a no-fault) state. You forget that the primary purpose of Auto Insurance is to pay the large TORT claims and medical expenses for the "victims" in the accident. The actual physical responsibility is quite minimal. <P>State Farm lists my 85 SE 2.5L as "Coupe, 2dr" My rates for PL/PD are quite low ...  but then I'm old enough to remember when a Bonneville was a "concept car"!! Makes a big difference.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000018||Mach10||04-28-2001||10:48 PM||||**whistles happily to himself, safe and snug under a government-run, monopolistic public insurance plan**<P>I pay 800 per year, 200 deductable, 50,000 damage, 200,000 liability (bad record  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">) on an 86 SE, and 900 per year with the same plan on an 86 GT.<P>Don't believe me? Go to <BR> <A HREF="http://www.mpi.mb.ca/IRC/intro.asp?Lang=0" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mpi.mb.ca/IRC/intro.asp?Lang=0</A> <BR>you can use my old postal code if you'd like(r2n1w4) and play around with the insurance rates... btw, I fall under "No discount" on account of my record...<P>Why do americans fear monopolies so badly? The only downside to our system is the piss-poor customer service. For this kind of cheap insurance, I can live with that.||||reg||10
Z||000019||killerb15||04-29-2001||12:07 AM||||-oh man, I also live in Barrie, but don't yet have my license. I plan to buy my first car this fall (a fiero), but I didn't even plan to spens $4200 on it, let alone on insurance per year. I don't need to worry about insurance for awhile yet, but I was considering putting me as a "second driver" on my car, anyone done this, price? but if My parents insurance company won't let me, i'll be have the same problem as you. Please post if you find a company that is a good price. Good luck with your search.<P>-Brandon<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000020||Tom88gt||04-29-2001||09:14 PM||||What about a "liability bond"?  An aquaintance once mentioned that he has no auto insurance.  He bonded himself instead.  He has a Corvette, Jag, RV, a couple of other cars and 2 Harleys.  Yes, he is in his 50's, married with a couple of kids.  I'm not sure how that figures in.  I'm guessing his bond covers his whole family.  It covers him for any liability.  Whatever or whoever's car he is driving.  Boat accident.  If he kicks somebody's ass and gets sued.  His dog bits someone.  It basicaly covers him for everything and any vehicle he operates.  <P>In many states, you are required to show "proof of financial responsibility" not "insurance".  All that auto insurance is a bond to cover your ass.  But everyone uses insurance instead cause most people don't have that much cash on hand.<P>I don't think a liability bond is cheaper that car insurance.  Unless you are driving several cars.  I have 2 cars insured and the cost sucks.  I can only drive one at a time but I still pay for both.  A bond should be the same cost, one vehicle or ten.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000021||fyerstarter||04-29-2001||10:11 PM||||That sounds like a good idea to me. At least if you don't get in an accident then at some point you probably get your money back.||||reg||1
Z||000022||AkursedX||04-29-2001||10:56 PM||||I'm on insurance with my mother with State Farm.  I am 20 with a clean driving record.  I have like 150k in liability and nothing else and I pay 130 a month for my fiero and 115 a month for my duster.  But I get a big discount for being a college student with a 3.0 GPA or better.  It's the only reason I really try in school, lol||||reg||1
Z||000023||GoCartGuy||04-29-2001||10:58 PM||||Well to all of you who think insurance is not necessary, better pray you don't hit a deer in your Fiero.  I did $1200 in damage to mine this past December and I think God that I only have a $100 deductible for comprehensive.<P>Anyways, I'm 21 with a not too clean driving record, one accident, my fault for which I got reckless driving, and at the moment one ticket.  I pay $245 a month with USAA and that's for full coverage.  That means $100 deductible comprehensive (animals, downed trees, break ins, other acts of God) and $500 collision (I hit someone else), which in my opinion is worth it.  BTW USAA lists my 85 GT as a 2Dr Sport Coupe.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
